Test (#923): a --branch naming a ref that does not exist is never silently accepted — it exits non-zero, never reports sinked, closes no issue, advances no remote, and names the branch; TBD is not exempt
Test (#931 n0): arming control — closure-audit's summary-citation scanner really does flag a bare-relative .cache/ citation the archive does not hold, so an empty result in (n1)/(n2) is a measurement rather than a dead reader
Test (#931 n1): a collision with an UNTRACKED pre-existing archive — the run's only copy of that evidence — must be named in the committed record: the suffixed archive is committed as always, and the bytes it commits say which directory was already there
Test (#931 n2): the same disclosure is owed when the pre-existing archive is TRACKED — a report that only speaks up for the untracked shape leaves the other collision exactly as silent as before
Test (#931 n3): control — with NO pre-existing archive the sink must claim no collision, so the disclosure carries information instead of being unconditional boilerplate
Test (#931 n4): the disclosure must reach all FOUR sink copies — a gitlab-only or gitea-only omission is invisible to validate-script-sync.js and to edition-sync.js --check alike, so nothing but this would catch a three-copy fix
Test (#931 n5 root/collision): the collision statement in the committed record must track whether a prior archive was REALLY there — driven through this edition's own sink
